  "worked_solution": "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}={y}^{\u2032} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}={y}^{\u2033}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}={y}^{\u2033}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{\u2033}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}=-6{y}^{\u2032}+y+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}=-6{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}={x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
